1.e4 {This study examines another option at the 5th move, which is Qxe2. Let's see it!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures. We develop our knight.} 2...Nf6 3.d4 {White controls some central squares with d4. Developing with tempo is the way here!} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {White attacks our bishop in return. Retreating doesn't benefit us, so we exchange bishops.} 4...Bxe2 5.Qxe2 {This study covers 5.Qxe2, which is the second most popular move, occurring in 26% of the games. c4 is the threat, so we'll regain the pawn by capturing on d5, also hitting the g2-pawn.} 5...Qxd5 6.Nf3 {White covers it and prepares to castle. Let's open up our dark-squared bishop.} 6...e6 7.O-O {White castles. Black continues to develop.} 7...Nc6 8.c3 {White supports the d4-pawn with 8.c3. Black now develops and prepares to castle.} 8...Bd6 9.Nbd2 {White develops the knight to d2.} 9...Qh5 {The queen on h5 nicely supports the kingside attack, which will be initiated with ...g5 and ...g4.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Wuss Variation (4.Be2) / Study 2: The Queen Recapture (5.Qxe2) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/780abea8-4d32-4633-a19c-b4e90c3cc6b0/chapters/70b39ac4-caf8-4da4-b9ff-a66e58470975/studies/65448849-5be2-4474-8580-f11334c6a905/lines"]

1.e4 {This study examines another option at the 5th move, which is Qxe2. Let's see it!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures. We develop our knight.} 2...Nf6 3.d4 {White controls some central squares with d4. Developing with tempo is the way here!} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {White attacks our bishop in return. Retreating doesn't benefit us, so we exchange bishops.} 4...Bxe2 5.Qxe2 {This study covers 5.Qxe2, which is the second most popular move, occurring in 26% of the games. c4 is the threat, so we'll regain the pawn by capturing on d5, also hitting the g2-pawn.} 5...Qxd5 6.Nf3 {White covers it and prepares to castle. Let's open up our dark-squared bishop.} 6...e6 7.O-O {White castles. Black continues to develop.} 7...Nc6 8.c4 {In nearly half of the games, White attacks us with c4 here. Retreating would be passive, but we can grab on d4 with a counter-attack.} 8...Nxd4 9.Nxd4 {White trades. We'll recapture with the queen.} 9...Qxd4 10.Nc3 {White develops a minor piece instead of attacking our queen. The threat is Nb5. Let's prevent it.} 10...a6 11.Be3 {The bishop attacks our queen.} 11...Qg4 {White's pawn sacrifice didn't offer anything and we have a comfortable position after ...Qg4.} *

