1.e4 {This study examines another option at the 5th move, which is Qxe2. Let's see it!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures. We develop our knight.} 2...Nf6 3.d4 {White controls some central squares with d4. Developing with tempo is the way here!} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {White attacks our bishop in return. Retreating doesn't benefit us, so we exchange bishops.} 4...Bxe2 5.Qxe2 {This study covers 5.Qxe2, which is the second most popular move, occurring in 26% of the games. c4 is the threat, so we'll regain the pawn by capturing on d5, also hitting the g2-pawn.} 5...Qxd5 6.Nf3 {White covers it and prepares to castle. Let's open up our dark-squared bishop.} 6...e6 7.O-O {White castles. Black continues to develop.} 7...Nc6 8.c4 {In nearly half of the games, White attacks us with c4 here. Retreating would be passive, but we can grab on d4 with a counter-attack.} 8...Nxd4 9.Nxd4 {White trades. We'll recapture with the queen.} 9...Qxd4 10.Rd1 {White attacks our queen on d4.} 10...Qg4 {We'll move it to g4 and offer a queen trade. Black is better due to better-developed pieces and being a pawn up.} *

