1.e4 {This study examines another option at the 5th move, which is Qxe2. Let's see it!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures. We develop our knight.} 2...Nf6 3.d4 {White controls some central squares with d4. Developing with tempo is the way here!} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {White attacks our bishop in return. Retreating doesn't benefit us, so we exchange bishops.} 4...Bxe2 5.Qxe2 {This study covers 5.Qxe2, which is the second most popular move, occurring in 26% of the games. c4 is the threat, so we'll regain the pawn by capturing on d5, also hitting the g2-pawn.} 5...Qxd5 6.Nf3 {White covers it and prepares to castle. Let's open up our dark-squared bishop.} 6...e6 7.O-O {White castles. Black continues to develop.} 7...Nc6 8.Nc3 {White immediately develops and attacks our queen. The queen goes to a typical square.} 8...Qf5 9.Be3 {White develops and supports the d4-pawn.} 9...O-O-O {After castling queenside, we once again have ideas of attacking with ...g5, ...g4, this time supported with ...Rg8.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The True Portuguese (4.f3) / Study 1: White Plays 5.c4 and 5.Bd3  / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/780abea8-4d32-4633-a19c-b4e90c3cc6b0/chapters/58fd45f9-fea5-4f59-ba2d-426d83757ee5/studies/570d5f01-2420-4109-8dd3-b52162ae8630/lines"]

1.e4 {In the fourth chapter, White blocks our bishop's attack with 4.f3, and next goes for 5.c4 or 5.Bd3. Let's check it out!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes, as usual. We develop our knight.} 2...Nf6 3.d4 {White ignores their pawn on d5 and pushes d4. We develop our bishop with tempo, attacking the white queen.} 3...Bg4 4.f3 {White blocks with the f-pawn. We'll retreat but keep the bishop active.} 4...Bf5 5.c4 {This move occurs in 50% of the games. White tries to hold on to the d5-pawn by pushing c4. As we know from previous chapters, this structure is best challenged with ...e6.} 5...e6 6.dxe6 {80% of the time, White captures the e6-pawn! This plays into our hands. Now we have a true gambit on the board! We go for pure development.} 6...Nc6 7.exf7+ {White continues to grab the pawns, this time with a check. We don't mind our king being on f7. Black is far ahead in development as compensation.} 7...Kxf7 8.d5 {White tries to gain even more space in the center and attacks our knight. Now the active pieces show their potential. We threaten a knight fork on c2.} 8...Nb4 9.Na3 {White defends with the knight.} 9...Bc5 {White's king is in serious danger after ...Bc5. ...Re8+ is coming which will further exploit White's lack of development.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The True Portuguese (4.f3) / Study 1: White Plays 5.c4 and 5.Bd3  / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/780abea8-4d32-4633-a19c-b4e90c3cc6b0/chapters/58fd45f9-fea5-4f59-ba2d-426d83757ee5/studies/570d5f01-2420-4109-8dd3-b52162ae8630/lines"]

1.e4 {In the fourth chapter, White blocks our bishop's attack with 4.f3, and next goes for 5.c4 or 5.Bd3. Let's check it out!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes, as usual. We develop our knight.} 2...Nf6 3.d4 {White ignores their pawn on d5 and pushes d4. We develop our bishop with tempo, attacking the white queen.} 3...Bg4 4.f3 {White blocks with the f-pawn. We'll retreat but keep the bishop active.} 4...Bf5 5.c4 {This move occurs in 50% of the games. White tries to hold on to the d5-pawn by pushing c4. As we know from previous chapters, this structure is best challenged with ...e6.} 5...e6 6.dxe6 {80% of the time, White captures the e6-pawn! This plays into our hands. Now we have a true gambit on the board! We go for pure development.} 6...Nc6 7.d5 {White connects the pawns and attacks our knight with d5. The knight finds its active square on b4.} 7...Nb4 8.Na3 {The knight defends the fork.} 8...Bc5 {The bishop pair is slicing through White's camp, making the king feel very unsafe. The center will open up soon, which only benefits us.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The True Portuguese (4.f3) / Study 1: White Plays 5.c4 and 5.Bd3  / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/780abea8-4d32-4633-a19c-b4e90c3cc6b0/chapters/58fd45f9-fea5-4f59-ba2d-426d83757ee5/studies/570d5f01-2420-4109-8dd3-b52162ae8630/lines"]

1.e4 {In the fourth chapter, White blocks our bishop's attack with 4.f3, and next goes for 5.c4 or 5.Bd3. Let's check it out!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes, as usual. We develop our knight.} 2...Nf6 3.d4 {White ignores their pawn on d5 and pushes d4. We develop our bishop with tempo, attacking the white queen.} 3...Bg4 4.f3 {White blocks with the f-pawn. We'll retreat but keep the bishop active.} 4...Bf5 5.c4 {This move occurs in 50% of the games. White tries to hold on to the d5-pawn by pushing c4. As we know from previous chapters, this structure is best challenged with ...e6.} 5...e6 6.g4 {6.g4 is aggressive and the machine's top choice, but has some flaws. Now things get very tricky. We'll sacrifice the knight on g4.} 6...Nxg4 7.fxg4 {The point of this sacrifice was to open the h4-d8 diagonal for our queen.} 7...Qh4+ 8.Kd2 {White moves the king. The bishop is hanging, so let's improve it by attacking the rook in the corner.} 8...Be4 9.Nf3 {White blocks the bishop's line of fire with the knight.} 9...Qf2+ {But we win the piece back with ...Qf2+.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The True Portuguese (4.f3) / Study 1: White Plays 5.c4 and 5.Bd3  / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/780abea8-4d32-4633-a19c-b4e90c3cc6b0/chapters/58fd45f9-fea5-4f59-ba2d-426d83757ee5/studies/570d5f01-2420-4109-8dd3-b52162ae8630/lines"]

1.e4 {In the fourth chapter, White blocks our bishop's attack with 4.f3, and next goes for 5.c4 or 5.Bd3. Let's check it out!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes, as usual. We develop our knight.} 2...Nf6 3.d4 {White ignores their pawn on d5 and pushes d4. We develop our bishop with tempo, attacking the white queen.} 3...Bg4 4.f3 {White blocks with the f-pawn. We'll retreat but keep the bishop active.} 4...Bf5 5.Bd3 {This is the second most popular move, played in 14% of the games. White's idea is to trade off our most active piece. Retreating doesn't give us anything, so we'll exchange.} 5...Bxd3 6.Qxd3 {The queen recaptures.} 6...Nxd5 {And after we grab the pawn back, we'll simply continue our development with ...e6 and ...Nc6.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The True Portuguese (4.f3) / Study 2: White Plays 5.g4, 5.Nc3 or 5.Bb5+ / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/780abea8-4d32-4633-a19c-b4e90c3cc6b0/chapters/58fd45f9-fea5-4f59-ba2d-426d83757ee5/studies/a93e7bd5-bb2b-4b88-a586-0d6c7ad77ee8/lines"]

1.e4 {The second study of the fourth chapter covers further fifth moves by White.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White often captures the pawn on d5. We develop our knight.} 2...Nf6 3.d4 {In 23% of the games, White pushes d4. We'll develop our bishop with gain of tempo on the white queen.} 3...Bg4 4.f3 {White blocks the scope of our bishop with f3. f5 is the square.} 4...Bf5 5.Bb5+ {In around 10% of the games, White gives a check on b5, arguing that they are not afraid of c6 in reply. We'll interpose with the b8-knight.} 5...Nbd7 6.Nc3 {White prefers to develop the knight instead. ...c6 isn't possible to kick the bishop, so we'll opt for another pawn push.} 6...a6 7.Ba4 {White retreats. Black can gain some more tempi on the bishop.} 7...b5 8.Bb3 {White's only move. We can now add pressure to the d5-pawn, as it hinders our development.} 8...Nb6 9.Nge2 {White develops the knight, preparing to castle. We've built up some space on the queenside. Let's continue.} 9...a5 10.a4 {White prevents ...a4 by pushing a4 themselves.} 10...b4 {We just keep gaining queenside space with ...b4. The d5-pawn will fall shortly.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The True Portuguese (4.f3) / Study 2: White Plays 5.g4, 5.Nc3 or 5.Bb5+ / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/780abea8-4d32-4633-a19c-b4e90c3cc6b0/chapters/58fd45f9-fea5-4f59-ba2d-426d83757ee5/studies/a93e7bd5-bb2b-4b88-a586-0d6c7ad77ee8/lines"]

1.e4 {The second study of the fourth chapter covers further fifth moves by White.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White often captures the pawn on d5. We develop our knight.} 2...Nf6 3.d4 {In 23% of the games, White pushes d4. We'll develop our bishop with gain of tempo on the white queen.} 3...Bg4 4.f3 {White blocks the scope of our bishop with f3. f5 is the square.} 4...Bf5 5.Bb5+ {In around 10% of the games, White gives a check on b5, arguing that they are not afraid of c6 in reply. We'll interpose with the b8-knight.} 5...Nbd7 6.c4 {White strengthens the pawn structure with c4. The reply is consistent with the previous lines.} 6...e6 7.dxe6 {White trades. Let's recapture, but keep the f5-bishop active.} 7...fxe6 8.Ne2 {White prepares to castle. The pin is quite annoying, so let's get rid of it.} 8...c6 9.Ba4 {White retreats to a4.} 9...Qc7 {The queen belongs on c7, from where it supports the ...e5-push and allows us to castle queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The True Portuguese (4.f3) / Study 2: White Plays 5.g4, 5.Nc3 or 5.Bb5+ / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/780abea8-4d32-4633-a19c-b4e90c3cc6b0/chapters/58fd45f9-fea5-4f59-ba2d-426d83757ee5/studies/a93e7bd5-bb2b-4b88-a586-0d6c7ad77ee8/lines"]

1.e4 {The second study of the fourth chapter covers further fifth moves by White.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White often captures the pawn on d5. We develop our knight.} 2...Nf6 3.d4 {In 23% of the games, White pushes d4. We'll develop our bishop with gain of tempo on the white queen.} 3...Bg4 4.f3 {White blocks the scope of our bishop with f3. f5 is the square.} 4...Bf5 5.g4 {This is the third most popular move, occurring in 11% of the games. It's the engine's top suggestion but White's position will have many holes in practice. We simply retreat the bishop.} 5...Bg6 6.c4 {White strengthens the pawn on d5. This is answered by e6, targeting their central pawns.} 6...e6 7.dxe6 {White decides to grab on e6. Our opponent hasn't developed their pieces yet, so we can afford to gambit on f7. We continue our minor piece development.} 7...Nc6 8.exf7+ {White continues to capture pawns.} 8...Kxf7 {Yet again, our king is safe on f7 and we have plans of ...Nb4/targeting the d4-pawn. White hasn't moved any of their pieces so far!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The True Portuguese (4.f3) / Study 2: White Plays 5.g4, 5.Nc3 or 5.Bb5+ / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/780abea8-4d32-4633-a19c-b4e90c3cc6b0/chapters/58fd45f9-fea5-4f59-ba2d-426d83757ee5/studies/a93e7bd5-bb2b-4b88-a586-0d6c7ad77ee8/lines"]

1.e4 {The second study of the fourth chapter covers further fifth moves by White.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White often captures the pawn on d5. We develop our knight.} 2...Nf6 3.d4 {In 23% of the games, White pushes d4. We'll develop our bishop with gain of tempo on the white queen.} 3...Bg4 4.f3 {White blocks the scope of our bishop with f3. f5 is the square.} 4...Bf5 5.g4 {This is the third most popular move, occurring in 11% of the games. It's the engine's top suggestion but White's position will have many holes in practice. We simply retreat the bishop.} 5...Bg6 6.c4 {White strengthens the pawn on d5. This is answered by e6, targeting their central pawns.} 6...e6 7.Nc3 {White doesn't trade and develops the knight instead. Now that White opts for development, gambiting on e6 isn't worth it anymore, so we'll simply take on d5.} 7...exd5 8.g5 {White pushes g5 and attacks our knight. ...Nh5 is the most logical.} 8...Nh5 9.f4 {9.f4 is a great move that occurs in 40% of the games. White aims to push f5 and discoordinate our pieces. Let's check out how to counter it.} 9...Bb4 10.f5 {White advances with f5. We have to capture it.} 10...Bxf5 11.Qxh5 {White has gained a piece...} 11...O-O {...but after we castle, we're ahead in development and White's king is unsafe, which gives us good compensation for the piece.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The True Portuguese (4.f3) / Study 2: White Plays 5.g4, 5.Nc3 or 5.Bb5+ / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/780abea8-4d32-4633-a19c-b4e90c3cc6b0/chapters/58fd45f9-fea5-4f59-ba2d-426d83757ee5/studies/a93e7bd5-bb2b-4b88-a586-0d6c7ad77ee8/lines"]

1.e4 {The second study of the fourth chapter covers further fifth moves by White.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White often captures the pawn on d5. We develop our knight.} 2...Nf6 3.d4 {In 23% of the games, White pushes d4. We'll develop our bishop with gain of tempo on the white queen.} 3...Bg4 4.f3 {White blocks the scope of our bishop with f3. f5 is the square.} 4...Bf5 5.g4 {This is the third most popular move, occurring in 11% of the games. It's the engine's top suggestion but White's position will have many holes in practice. We simply retreat the bishop.} 5...Bg6 6.c4 {White strengthens the pawn on d5. This is answered by e6, targeting their central pawns.} 6...e6 7.Nc3 {White doesn't trade and develops the knight instead. Now that White opts for development, gambiting on e6 isn't worth it anymore, so we'll simply take on d5.} 7...exd5 8.g5 {White pushes g5 and attacks our knight. ...Nh5 is the most logical.} 8...Nh5 9.Nxd5 {White regains the pawn on d5.} 9...Nc6 {We'll develop the knight to c6. This was one of the better ways to play this as White, but they still lack development, and we have the tidier pawn structure. Our plan will revolve around getting the pawn on d4 back and, if we get the chance, trading the queens into an endgame is also a good plan.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The True Portuguese (4.f3) / Study 2: White Plays 5.g4, 5.Nc3 or 5.Bb5+ / Variation 4"]
