1.e4 {The second study of the fourth chapter covers further fifth moves by White.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White often captures the pawn on d5. We develop our knight.} 2...Nf6 3.d4 {In 23% of the games, White pushes d4. We'll develop our bishop with gain of tempo on the white queen.} 3...Bg4 4.f3 {White blocks the scope of our bishop with f3. f5 is the square.} 4...Bf5 5.Nc3 {Very few games continue with 5.Nc3, as it's not a move that poses any threats. We can simply recapture on d5.} 5...Nxd5 6.Nxd5 {White trades, and we recapture.} 6...Qxd5 7.c4 {White gains tempo on our queen with c4.} 7...Qd7 {We just retreat to d7. We can continue our development with ...Nc6 and ...e6 to open up the dark-squared bishop.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Scandinavian Modern Variation (3.Nf3) / Study 1: White Plays 4.Be2 or 4.h3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/780abea8-4d32-4633-a19c-b4e90c3cc6b0/chapters/ea745126-5095-4de9-aaa1-153304da03f5/studies/ec8f9afa-e402-4c91-b8d2-cf850208f21c/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the fifth chapter! Here, White adapts a calmer approach with 3.Nf3 and just plays the Scandinavian Modern Variation. The following two studies examine White's possibilities on the fourth move. Let's jump right in!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White often trades. For deviations on this move check out the sixth chapter! We'll develop our knight, as usual.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White doesn't try to retain the d5-pawn and develops in a simple manner instead. Percentage-wise, this move occurs in 13% of the games, but that still represents over one million games. We'll develop our bishop, pinning the f3-knight.} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {White blocks with the pin, which they do in half of the games. Now is the best time to recapture on d5.} 4...Qxd5 5.O-O {White castles. We keep developing towards the center.} 5...Nc6 6.Nc3 {White develops with a tempo on our queen. We can keep it active by placing it on f5.} 6...Qf5 7.h3 {White now attacks our bishop on g4. The bishop won't be useful in the future, so let's trade it off on f3.} 7...Bxf3 8.Bxf3 {White recaptures. We'll make room for our dark-squared bishop to emerge.} 8...e6 9.d3 {White doesn't go for d4, so the d-pawn is not a target for us in this variation.} 9...Bd6 {The bishop gets developed to d6 with a kingside pawn storm coming!} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Scandinavian Modern Variation (3.Nf3) / Study 1: White Plays 4.Be2 or 4.h3 / Variation 2"]
