1.e4 {Welcome to the fifth chapter! Here, White adapts a calmer approach with 3.Nf3 and just plays the Scandinavian Modern Variation. The following two studies examine White's possibilities on the fourth move. Let's jump right in!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White often trades. For deviations on this move check out the sixth chapter! We'll develop our knight, as usual.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White doesn't try to retain the d5-pawn and develops in a simple manner instead. Percentage-wise, this move occurs in 13% of the games, but that still represents over one million games. We'll develop our bishop, pinning the f3-knight.} 3...Bg4 4.h3 {White rarely doesn't unpin themselves with Be2. Here, White attacks our bishop, but we can maintain the pin.} 4...Bh5 5.g4 {White keeps attacking our bishop, but weakens their kingside somewhat. We'll retreat to g6. Sacrificing on g4 wouldn't be a good idea.} 5...Bg6 6.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3.} 6...Nxd5 {We can recapture the pawn now. The standard moves like ...e6 and ...Nc6 are coming. White's h3 and g4 pawns could potentially become weak, which could be exploited with ...h5.} *

