1.e4 {Welcome to the fifth chapter! Here, White adapts a calmer approach with 3.Nf3 and just plays the Scandinavian Modern Variation. The following two studies examine White's possibilities on the fourth move. Let's jump right in!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White often trades. For deviations on this move check out the sixth chapter! We'll develop our knight, as usual.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White doesn't try to retain the d5-pawn and develops in a simple manner instead. Percentage-wise, this move occurs in 13% of the games, but that still represents over one million games. We'll develop our bishop, pinning the f3-knight.} 3...Bg4 4.h3 {White rarely doesn't unpin themselves with Be2. Here, White attacks our bishop, but we can maintain the pin.} 4...Bh5 5.Be2 {In 23% of the games White unpins themselves with Be2.} 5...Nxd5 {We'll simply recapture on d5 with the knight with ...e6 and ...Nc6 development coming.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Scandinavian Modern Variation (3.Nf3) / Study 2: White Plays 4.d4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/780abea8-4d32-4633-a19c-b4e90c3cc6b0/chapters/ea745126-5095-4de9-aaa1-153304da03f5/studies/645da64a-618e-4b98-8b6c-a9a549e6e706/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of the fifth chapter! Last time we examined 4.Be2 and 4.h3. Here, White goes for 4.d4, occupying the center. Let's check it out!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White trades. We develop our knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White develops as well with 3.Nf3. We develop our bishop, pinning the knight.} 3...Bg4 4.d4 {White opts for the second most popular move, which happens 20% of the time, and aims to control some central squares. c4 could be a threat, so let's grab on d5.} 4...Qxd5 5.Be2 {White unpins themselves and prepares to castle. Time for our knight to put some pressure on the center.} 5...Nc6 6.O-O {In 21% of the games, White goes for kingside castling. We'll castle as well, while pressuring d4.} 6...O-O-O 7.c4 {White attacks our queen. Since we've castled queenside, kingside attack is logical, and we place the queen accordingly on h5.} 7...Qh5 8.d5 {White expands immediately with d5.} 8...e6 {It doesn't threaten anything because it's pinned and ...e6 just adds pressure to it.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Scandinavian Modern Variation (3.Nf3) / Study 2: White Plays 4.d4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/780abea8-4d32-4633-a19c-b4e90c3cc6b0/chapters/ea745126-5095-4de9-aaa1-153304da03f5/studies/645da64a-618e-4b98-8b6c-a9a549e6e706/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of the fifth chapter! Last time we examined 4.Be2 and 4.h3. Here, White goes for 4.d4, occupying the center. Let's check it out!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White trades. We develop our knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White develops as well with 3.Nf3. We develop our bishop, pinning the knight.} 3...Bg4 4.d4 {White opts for the second most popular move, which happens 20% of the time, and aims to control some central squares. c4 could be a threat, so let's grab on d5.} 4...Qxd5 5.Be2 {White unpins themselves and prepares to castle. Time for our knight to put some pressure on the center.} 5...Nc6 6.O-O {In 21% of the games, White goes for kingside castling. We'll castle as well, while pressuring d4.} 6...O-O-O 7.c4 {White attacks our queen. Since we've castled queenside, kingside attack is logical, and we place the queen accordingly on h5.} 7...Qh5 8.Be3 {White develops the dark-squared bishop. Can you spot the strong move that utilizes our d8-rook?} 8...e5 9.d5 {White gets into trouble after d5, since it allows us to keep attacking by playing e4.} 9...e4 10.Nd4 {White jumps to d4 and saves their knight. Now the c6-knight is under attack as White blocked the pin on the d-file. We'll capture on d4.} 10...Nxd4 11.Bxg4+ {White captures on g4 with a check. We'll recapture with the knight, threatening mate on h2!} 11...Nxg4 12.h3 {White prevents it. But now we have a surprising way to continue the attack!} 12...Nf3+ 13.gxf3 {White recaptures. Now their king doesn't have any protection. If Kh1 instead, we'd have ...Qe5.} 13...Ne5 {We'll retreat the knight to e5. A huge attack is coming with ...Rd6 to ...Rg6.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Scandinavian Modern Variation (3.Nf3) / Study 2: White Plays 4.d4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/780abea8-4d32-4633-a19c-b4e90c3cc6b0/chapters/ea745126-5095-4de9-aaa1-153304da03f5/studies/645da64a-618e-4b98-8b6c-a9a549e6e706/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of the fifth chapter! Last time we examined 4.Be2 and 4.h3. Here, White goes for 4.d4, occupying the center. Let's check it out!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White trades. We develop our knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White develops as well with 3.Nf3. We develop our bishop, pinning the knight.} 3...Bg4 4.d4 {White opts for the second most popular move, which happens 20% of the time, and aims to control some central squares. c4 could be a threat, so let's grab on d5.} 4...Qxd5 5.Be2 {White unpins themselves and prepares to castle. Time for our knight to put some pressure on the center.} 5...Nc6 6.O-O {In 21% of the games, White goes for kingside castling. We'll castle as well, while pressuring d4.} 6...O-O-O 7.c4 {White attacks our queen. Since we've castled queenside, kingside attack is logical, and we place the queen accordingly on h5.} 7...Qh5 8.h3 {Instead of Be3, White attacks our bishop with h3. Simply retreating is too passive and ...Bxh3 doesn't work. But we have a tactical trick by capturing on d4!} 8...Nxd4 9.Nxd4 {White trades on d4, since we were threatening ...Nxf3+. We'll exchange bishops and ultimately recover the piece.} 9...Bxe2 10.Qxe2 {White recaptures.} 10...Rxd4 {And we get the piece back. With an extra pawn, Black is doing great here.} *

