1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of the fifth chapter! Last time we examined 4.Be2 and 4.h3. Here, White goes for 4.d4, occupying the center. Let's check it out!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White trades. We develop our knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White develops as well with 3.Nf3. We develop our bishop, pinning the knight.} 3...Bg4 4.d4 {White opts for the second most popular move, which happens 20% of the time, and aims to control some central squares. c4 could be a threat, so let's grab on d5.} 4...Qxd5 5.Be2 {White unpins themselves and prepares to castle. Time for our knight to put some pressure on the center.} 5...Nc6 6.O-O {In 21% of the games, White goes for kingside castling. We'll castle as well, while pressuring d4.} 6...O-O-O 7.c4 {White attacks our queen. Since we've castled queenside, kingside attack is logical, and we place the queen accordingly on h5.} 7...Qh5 8.h3 {Instead of Be3, White attacks our bishop with h3. Simply retreating is too passive and ...Bxh3 doesn't work. But we have a tactical trick by capturing on d4!} 8...Nxd4 9.hxg4 {On hxg4...} 9...Nxg4 {...we recapture with the knight and White's position is hard to defend due to two threats: a check which also discovers a attack on the white queen, and checkmate on h2.} *

