1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of the fifth chapter! Last time we examined 4.Be2 and 4.h3. Here, White goes for 4.d4, occupying the center. Let's check it out!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White trades. We develop our knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White develops as well with 3.Nf3. We develop our bishop, pinning the knight.} 3...Bg4 4.d4 {White opts for the second most popular move, which happens 20% of the time, and aims to control some central squares. c4 could be a threat, so let's grab on d5.} 4...Qxd5 5.Be2 {White unpins themselves and prepares to castle. Time for our knight to put some pressure on the center.} 5...Nc6 6.c4 {White attacks our queen which is most common with a 30% occurrence.} 6...Qf5 {After ...Qf5, our typical plan of ...O-O-O and ...e5 applies.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Early Deviations / Study 1: White Plays 3.Bb5+ or 3.Bc4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/780abea8-4d32-4633-a19c-b4e90c3cc6b0/chapters/0b9b0486-b8d2-47f3-b5d6-0226e5d39de0/studies/8d380375-a243-46ca-a46c-a3aa1747c109/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the sixth chapter! This chapter examines White's early deviations on the third move. The first study covers 3.Bb5+ and 3.Bc4, which are both some of the least popular moves. Let's check it out!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures on d5. We develop our knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Bb5+ {Less than 10% of the games continue with 3.Bb5+. We can simply block with the knight.} 3...Nbd7 4.c4 {White supports the d5-pawn. Let's ask the bishop a question.} 4...a6 5.Bxd7+ {White decides to trade off the light-squared bishops.} 5...Qxd7 {It'll be recaptured with the queen and our plans include pushing ...e6 and ...b5. We already have a lead of two development tempi in return for our pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Early Deviations / Study 1: White Plays 3.Bb5+ or 3.Bc4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/780abea8-4d32-4633-a19c-b4e90c3cc6b0/chapters/0b9b0486-b8d2-47f3-b5d6-0226e5d39de0/studies/8d380375-a243-46ca-a46c-a3aa1747c109/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the sixth chapter! This chapter examines White's early deviations on the third move. The first study covers 3.Bb5+ and 3.Bc4, which are both some of the least popular moves. Let's check it out!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures on d5. We develop our knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Bb5+ {Less than 10% of the games continue with 3.Bb5+. We can simply block with the knight.} 3...Nbd7 4.c4 {White supports the d5-pawn. Let's ask the bishop a question.} 4...a6 5.Ba4 {White chooses to retreat, maintaining the pin, which gives us additional tempo. We'll play dynamically and undermine the central white pawns with the thrust ...b5.} 5...b5 6.cxb5 {White wins the b-pawn, but gives us some activity. We'll capture on d5.} 6...Nxd5 7.bxa6 {White continues to grab material.} 7...Bxa6 {This helps our light-squared bishop to develop to a strong square on a6. Ideas of ...Nb4 and ...Nd3+ are in the air!} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Early Deviations / Study 1: White Plays 3.Bb5+ or 3.Bc4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/780abea8-4d32-4633-a19c-b4e90c3cc6b0/chapters/0b9b0486-b8d2-47f3-b5d6-0226e5d39de0/studies/8d380375-a243-46ca-a46c-a3aa1747c109/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the sixth chapter! This chapter examines White's early deviations on the third move. The first study covers 3.Bb5+ and 3.Bc4, which are both some of the least popular moves. Let's check it out!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures on d5. We develop our knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Bb5+ {Less than 10% of the games continue with 3.Bb5+. We can simply block with the knight.} 3...Nbd7 4.Nc3 {In 30% of the games, White develops the knight to c3 here, not going for c4. We will still pose a question to the bishop, meaning that we'll be able to push ...b5 without any problems!} 4...a6 5.Ba4 {The bishop keeps its pin. Let's gain some space and time with ...b5.} 5...b5 6.Bb3 {The only move that doesn't lose material. The bishop on b3 is quite strong, but we can get rid of it.} 6...Nc5 7.Qf3 {White lines up their queen with the rook on a8...} 7...Bb7 {...but we just fianchetto and cover the diagonal. The d5-pawn is a target!} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Early Deviations / Study 1: White Plays 3.Bb5+ or 3.Bc4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/780abea8-4d32-4633-a19c-b4e90c3cc6b0/chapters/0b9b0486-b8d2-47f3-b5d6-0226e5d39de0/studies/8d380375-a243-46ca-a46c-a3aa1747c109/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the sixth chapter! This chapter examines White's early deviations on the third move. The first study covers 3.Bb5+ and 3.Bc4, which are both some of the least popular moves. Let's check it out!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures on d5. We develop our knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Bc4 {Over 1,000,000 games are played with 3.Bc4. The d5-pawn can simply be captured.} 3...Nxd5 4.Nf3 {20% of the time, White develops the knight here. We will pin the f3-knight to the queen.} 4...Bg4 5.O-O {White castles. ...e6 will support our central knight and open up lines for our pieces.} 5...e6 6.h3 {White attacks our bishop on g4. Here we generally trade on f3.} 6...Bxf3 7.Qxf3 {White recaptures.} 7...Nc6 {And we continue our development with ...Nc6.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Early Deviations / Study 1: White Plays 3.Bb5+ or 3.Bc4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/780abea8-4d32-4633-a19c-b4e90c3cc6b0/chapters/0b9b0486-b8d2-47f3-b5d6-0226e5d39de0/studies/8d380375-a243-46ca-a46c-a3aa1747c109/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the sixth chapter! This chapter examines White's early deviations on the third move. The first study covers 3.Bb5+ and 3.Bc4, which are both some of the least popular moves. Let's check it out!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures on d5. We develop our knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Bc4 {Over 1,000,000 games are played with 3.Bc4. The d5-pawn can simply be captured.} 3...Nxd5 4.Bxd5 {In a quarter of the games, White trades on d5.} 4...Qxd5 5.Nf3 {And after Nf3...} 5...Bg4 {...our pieces get developed promptly, starting with ...Bg4 to pin the knight.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Early Deviations / Study 1: White Plays 3.Bb5+ or 3.Bc4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/780abea8-4d32-4633-a19c-b4e90c3cc6b0/chapters/0b9b0486-b8d2-47f3-b5d6-0226e5d39de0/studies/8d380375-a243-46ca-a46c-a3aa1747c109/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the sixth chapter! This chapter examines White's early deviations on the third move. The first study covers 3.Bb5+ and 3.Bc4, which are both some of the least popular moves. Let's check it out!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures on d5. We develop our knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Bc4 {Over 1,000,000 games are played with 3.Bc4. The d5-pawn can simply be captured.} 3...Nxd5 4.Qf3 {White adds pressure to the knight.} 4...Nf6 {We'll retreat to f6, cover the f7-pawn and prepare to continue developing with ...Nc6 and ...Bg4.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Early Deviations / Study 2: White Plays 2.e5, 2.Nf3 or 2.Nc3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/780abea8-4d32-4633-a19c-b4e90c3cc6b0/chapters/0b9b0486-b8d2-47f3-b5d6-0226e5d39de0/studies/dce31c7c-4015-4791-b031-de27ddafb60a/lines"]

1.e4 {The second study of this chapter covers completely different moves, where White ignores our pawn on d5. Let's see which other moves White could go for!} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {In 8% of the games, White has played 2.Nf3. There's no reason not to capture on e4.} 2...dxe4 3.Ng5 {White tries to regain it after jumping to g5, but we can develop and protect it.} 3...Bf5 4.f3 {A few of the games continue with 4.f3. White plays in a gambit style, going for a quick development themselves. We'll gladly accept, as this approach could backfire.} 4...exf3 5.Qxf3 {White recaptures with the queen, attacking both the bishop and the b7-pawn. No worries here. We'll simply cover the bishop!} 5...e6 6.Qxb7 {White goes after the b-pawn. Now we cover the rook.} 6...Nd7 7.Bb5 {White pins the knight and threatens Bxd7+. We have to respond with a counter-attack.} 7...Rb8 8.Bxd7+ {Since we cannot recapture with the queen, White trades on d7.} 8...Kxd7 9.Qxa7 {White saves the queen and goes after the a7-pawn, keeping an eye on the rook. Our king is safe, but White's isn't, which means that we can afford to sacrifice some more material.} 9...Qxg5 10.Qxb8 {White goes after the material gains. We'll go after the white king!} 10...Qh4+ 11.Kf1 {White moves to f1. Let's get the queen even closer to White's king.} 11...Qf4+ 12.Ke1 {They go to e1. We'll keep checking the king and support a future ...Bc5 to bring more pieces into the attack.} 12...Qe5+ 13.Kf1 {The king goes to f1.} 13...Bxc2 {And after ...Bxc2, with ...Bd3+ and Bc5+ to come, White's king soon finds itself in a mating net.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Early Deviations / Study 2: White Plays 2.e5, 2.Nf3 or 2.Nc3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/780abea8-4d32-4633-a19c-b4e90c3cc6b0/chapters/0b9b0486-b8d2-47f3-b5d6-0226e5d39de0/studies/dce31c7c-4015-4791-b031-de27ddafb60a/lines"]

1.e4 {The second study of this chapter covers completely different moves, where White ignores our pawn on d5. Let's see which other moves White could go for!} 1...d5 2.e5 {White decides to push e5 here, going for d4 next and gaining central space. An 11% occurrence represents over 40 million games! We'll make it harder for White to create a center with e5 and d4.} 2...c5 3.d4 {White goes for d4. We'll capture.} 3...cxd4 4.Qxd4 {White recaptures with the queen. Now the c-pawn is gone, we'll develop the knight, attacking the queen.} 4...Nc6 5.Bb5 {White pins us.} 5...Bf5 {But we just continue to develop with ...Bf5. ...e6 with ...Ne7 is next to continue getting our pieces out and play this position, which resembles the Caro-Kann.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Early Deviations / Study 2: White Plays 2.e5, 2.Nf3 or 2.Nc3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/780abea8-4d32-4633-a19c-b4e90c3cc6b0/chapters/0b9b0486-b8d2-47f3-b5d6-0226e5d39de0/studies/dce31c7c-4015-4791-b031-de27ddafb60a/lines"]

1.e4 {The second study of this chapter covers completely different moves, where White ignores our pawn on d5. Let's see which other moves White could go for!} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {In 8% of the games, White has played 2.Nf3. There's no reason not to capture on e4.} 2...dxe4 3.Ng5 {White tries to regain it after jumping to g5, but we can develop and protect it.} 3...Bf5 4.Nc3 {White continues to assault our pawn. Our approach stays the same.} 4...Nf6 5.Qe2 {White adds another attacker. ...Qd4 runs into Nb5, but we have another resource.} 5...Nc6 6.Ngxe4 {White recaptures on e4.} 6...Nd4 {But after ...Nd4, White has a few problems to solve. The ...Nxc2 fork is an immediate threat, which, for example, Qd1 doesn't solve due to the knight on e4 hanging.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Early Deviations / Study 2: White Plays 2.e5, 2.Nf3 or 2.Nc3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/780abea8-4d32-4633-a19c-b4e90c3cc6b0/chapters/0b9b0486-b8d2-47f3-b5d6-0226e5d39de0/studies/dce31c7c-4015-4791-b031-de27ddafb60a/lines"]

1.e4 {The second study of this chapter covers completely different moves, where White ignores our pawn on d5. Let's see which other moves White could go for!} 1...d5 2.Nc3 {The least played move of the three is 2.Nc3. Black can opt for a comfortable continuation by trading on e4.} 2...dxe4 3.Nxe4 {White recaptures. We'll challenge the knight with ...Nf6 and ask White what will they do with their e4-knight.} 3...Nf6 4.Nxf6+ {White trades.} 4...exf6 {And we recapture after which simple development follows.} *